WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.126 --> 00:00:03.211
[music]

2
00:00:05.422 --> 00:00:08.133
[Instructor] I hope you're ready.
Because we are about to get started

3
00:00:08.133 --> 00:00:10.051
on Unit 4.

4
00:00:10.051 --> 00:00:12.178
Now I know there are
a lot of people out there

5
00:00:12.178 --> 00:00:14.556
who say that this Unit
is the toughest one

6
00:00:14.556 --> 00:00:16.391
in this entire course.

7
00:00:16.391 --> 00:00:19.269
But you made it through Unit 3,
and that was no walk in the park,

8
00:00:19.728 --> 00:00:21.730
so I think you're ready.

9
00:00:22.022 --> 00:00:25.775
In this Unit, we will program
an arcade style sumo battle,

10
00:00:26.109 --> 00:00:28.236
with the Objective
of knocking waves

11
00:00:28.236 --> 00:00:32.115
of increasingly numerous enemies
off of a floating island.

12
00:00:33.241 --> 00:00:36.369
The Player will have to use
powerups in a desperate effort

13
00:00:36.453 --> 00:00:38.830
to withstand their relentless onslaught.

14
00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:42.000
If it sound dramatic,
that's because it is.

15
00:00:42.625 --> 00:00:45.670
In creating this Prototype,
you will learn how to introduce

16
00:00:45.670 --> 00:00:48.089
new "Gameplay Mechanics"
into your projects.

17
00:00:49.299 --> 00:00:52.927
A Gameplay Mechanic is a rule
or system in your game

18
00:00:53.178 --> 00:00:56.097
that ideally makes
it more interesting to play.

19
00:00:57.307 --> 00:01:00.185
One new mechanic you'll learn
to program is a Powerup,

20
00:01:00.977 --> 00:01:04.230
a temporary state that will
give the Player an advantage.

21
00:01:05.023 --> 00:01:07.400
These are not only super fun
for the Player to use,

22
00:01:07.734 --> 00:01:10.403
but can completely change
the way a game is played.

23
00:01:11.112 --> 00:01:13.239
Now the user needs to decide,

24
00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:15.408
should I go for the Powerup now?

25
00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:17.535
Maybe I should save the Powerup.

26
00:01:17.535 --> 00:01:19.537
What should I do
when I get the Powerup?

27
00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.249
All of these new questions,
and strategies will suddenly emerge,

28
00:01:23.583 --> 00:01:25.585
and make your game
way more interesting.

29
00:01:26.544 --> 00:01:28.755
The other mechanic
we'll introduce is

30
00:01:28.755 --> 00:01:31.966
if you defeat all the enemies
a new wave will appear

31
00:01:31.966 --> 00:01:34.427
with one more enemy
than the previous wave,

32
00:01:34.677 --> 00:01:36.721
Increasing the Difficulty over time.

33
00:01:37.764 --> 00:01:39.557
This will also completely change the way

34
00:01:39.557 --> 00:01:43.478
the game can be played because,
instead of being a static experience

35
00:01:43.478 --> 00:01:46.106
that the Player can quickly master,
and become bored of,

36
00:01:46.731 --> 00:01:49.150
it has limitless potential
to challenge

37
00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:51.194
even the most expert Players.

38
00:01:52.028 --> 00:01:53.905
These two Gameplay Mechanics,

39
00:01:53.905 --> 00:01:57.325
Powerups, and "Increasing
Difficulty" go hand and hand

40
00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.537
because as the game
increases in difficulty

41
00:02:00.912 --> 00:02:03.331
you can introduce new
Powerups to match it.

42
00:02:03.790 --> 00:02:07.585
This combination basically
removes all limitations

43
00:02:07.585 --> 00:02:09.504
from your project development

44
00:02:09.504 --> 00:02:12.006
because you can continually
up the ante

45
00:02:12.006 --> 00:02:15.093
increasing one and then increasing
the other in response.

46
00:02:15.885 --> 00:02:18.388
The sky's the limit here,
so lets start creating.

47
00:02:20.348 --> 00:02:22.475
[music]
